"inotify(7) - Linux manual page (https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/inotify.7.html)\nciteturn8view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view3\",\"lineno\":350}); Total lines: 748\nL323:                   an IN_Q_OVERFLOW event is always generated.\nL324: \nL325:            /proc/sys/fs/inotify/max_user_instances\nL326:                   This specifies an upper limit on the number of inotify\nL327:                   instances that can be created per real user ID.\nL328: \nL329:            /proc/sys/fs/inotify/max_user_watches\nL330:                   This specifies an upper limit on the number of watches that\nL331:                   can be created per real user ID.\nL332: ## STANDARDS         【12†top】\nL333: \nL334:            Linux.\nL335: \nL336: \nL337: ## HISTORY         【12†top】\nL338: \nL339:            Inotify was merged into Linux 2.6.13.  The required library\nL340:            interfaces were added in glibc 2.4.  (IN_DONT_FOLLOW, IN_MASK_ADD,\nL341:            and IN_ONLYDIR were added in glibc 2.5.)\nL342: ## NOTES         【12†top】\nL343: \nL344:            Inotify file descriptors can be monitored using 【34†select(2)】,\nL345:            【35†poll(2)】, and 【36†epoll(7)】.  When an event is available, the file\nL346:            descriptor indicates as readable.\nL347: \nL348:            Since Linux 2.6.25, signal-driven I/O notification is available\nL349:            for inotify file descriptors; see the discussion of F_SETFL (for\nL350:            setting the O_ASYNC flag), F_SETOWN, and F_SETSIG in 【37†fcntl(2)】.\nL351:            The siginfo_t structure (described in 【38†sigaction(2)】) that is passed\nL352:            to the signal handler has the following fields set: si_fd is set\nL353:            to the inotify file descriptor number; si_signo is set to the\nL354:            signal number; si_code is set to POLL_IN; and POLLIN is set in\nL355:            si_band.\nL356: \nL357:            If successive output inotify events produced on the inotify file\nL358:            descriptor are identical (same wd, mask, cookie, and name), then\nL359:            they are coalesced into a single event if the older event has not\nL360:            yet been read (but see BUGS).  This reduces the amount of kernel\nL361:            memory required for the event queue, but also means that an\nL362:            application can't use inotify to reliably count file events.\nL363: \nL364:            The events returned by reading from an inotify file descriptor\nL365:            form an ordered queue.  Thus, for example, it is guaranteed that\nL366:            when renaming from one directory to another, events will be\nL367:            produced in the correct order on the inotify file descriptor.\nL368: \nL369:            The set of watch descriptors that is being monitored via an\nL370:            inotify file descriptor can be viewed via the entry for the\nL371:            inotify file descriptor in the process's /proc/pid/fdinfo\nL372:            directory.  See 【39†proc(5)】 for further details.  The FIONREAD\nL373:            【40†ioctl(2)】 returns the number of bytes available to read from an\nL374:            inotify file descriptor.\nL375: \nL376:        Limitations and caveats\nL377:            The inotify API provides no information about the user or process\nL378:            that triggered the inotify event.  In particular, there is no easy\nL379:            way for a process that is monitoring events via inotify to\nL380:            distinguish events that it triggers itself from those that are\nL381:            triggered by other processes.\nL382:            Inotify reports only events that a user-space program triggers\nL383:            through the filesystem API.  As a result, it does not catch remote\nL384:            events that occur on network filesystems.  (Applications must fall\nL385:            back to polling the filesystem to catch such events.)\nL386:            Furthermore, various pseudo-filesystems such as /proc, /sys, and\nL387:            /dev/pts are not monitorable with inotify.\nL388: \nL389:            The inotify API does not report file accesses and modifications\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ninotify(7) - Linux manual page (https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/inotify.7.html)\nciteturn8view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view3\",\"lineno\":400}); Total lines: 748\nL382:            Inotify reports only events that a user-space program triggers\nL383:            through the filesystem API.  As a result, it does not catch remote\nL384:            events that occur on network filesystems.  (Applications must fall\nL385:            back to polling the filesystem to catch such events.)\nL386:            Furthermore, various pseudo-filesystems such as /proc, /sys, and\nL387:            /dev/pts are not monitorable with inotify.\nL388: \nL389:            The inotify API does not report file accesses and modifications\nL390:            that may occur because of 【41†mmap(2)】, 【42†msync(2)】, and 【43†munmap(2)】.\nL391: \nL392:            The inotify API identifies affected files by filename.  However,\nL393:            by the time an application processes an inotify event, the\nL394:            filename may already have been deleted or renamed.\nL395: \nL396:            The inotify API identifies events via watch descriptors.  It is\nL397:            the application's responsibility to cache a mapping (if one is\nL398:            needed) between watch descriptors and pathnames.  Be aware that\nL399:            directory renamings may affect multiple cached pathnames.\nL400: \nL401:            Inotify monitoring of directories is not recursive: to monitor\nL402:            subdirectories under a directory, additional watches must be\nL403:            created.  This can take a significant amount time for large\nL404:            directory trees.\nL405: \nL406:            If monitoring an entire directory subtree, and a new subdirectory\nL407:            is created in that tree or an existing directory is renamed into\nL408:            that tree, be aware that by the time you create a watch for the\nL409:            new subdirectory, new files (and subdirectories) may already exist\nL410:            inside the subdirectory.  Therefore, you may want to scan the\nL411:            contents of the subdirectory immediately after adding the watch\nL412:            (and, if desired, recursively add watches for any subdirectories\nL413:            that it contains).\nL414:            Note that the event queue can overflow.  In this case, events are\nL415:            lost.  Robust applications should handle the possibility of lost\nL416:            events gracefully.  For example, it may be necessary to rebuild\nL417:            part or all of the application cache.  (One simple, but possibly\nL418:            expensive, approach is to close the inotify file descriptor, empty\nL419:            the cache, create a new inotify file descriptor, and then re-\nL420:            create watches and cache entries for the objects to be monitored.)\nL421: \nL422:            If a filesystem is mounted on top of a monitored directory, no\nL423:            event is generated, and no events are generated for objects\nL424:            immediately under the new mount point.  If the filesystem is\nL425:            subsequently unmounted, events will subsequently be generated for\nL426:            the directory and the objects it contains.\nL427: \nL428:        Dealing with rename() events\nL429:            As noted above, the IN_MOVED_FROM and IN_MOVED_TO event pair that\nL430:            is generated by 【44†rename(2)】 can be matched up via their shared\nL431:            cookie value.  However, the task of matching has some challenges.\nL432: \nL433:            These two events are usually consecutive in the event stream\nL434:            available when reading from the inotify file descriptor.  However,\nL435:            this is not guaranteed.  If multiple processes are triggering\nL436:            events for monitored objects, then (on rare occasions) an\nL437:            arbitrary number of other events may appear between the\nL438:            IN_MOVED_FROM and IN_MOVED_TO events.  Furthermore, it is not\nL439:            guaranteed that the event pair is atomically inserted into the\nL440:            queue: there may be a brief interval where the IN_MOVED_FROM has\nL441:            appeared, but the IN_MOVED_TO has not.\nL442: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUsing the File System Events API (https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/Darwin/Conceptual/FSEvents_ProgGuide/UsingtheFSEventsFramework/UsingtheFSEventsFramework.html)\nciteturn8view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn7view1\",\"lineno\":101}); Total lines: 356\nL90: If you don’t already have a run loop, you will need to devote a thread to this task. After creating a thread using your API of choice, call `【13†CFRunLoopGetCurrent】` to allocate an initial run loop for that thread. Any future calls to `【13†CFRunLoopGetCurrent】` will return the same run loop.\nL91: For example, the following snippet shows how to schedule a stream, called `stream`, on the current thread’s run loop (not yet running):\nL92: \nL93:         FSEventStreamRef stream;\nL94: \nL95:         /* Create the stream before calling this. */\nL96: \nL97:         FSEventStreamScheduleWithRunLoop(stream, CFRunLoopGetCurrent(),         kCFRunLoopDefaultMode);\nL98: The final step in setting up an event stream is to call `FSEventStreamStart`. This function tells the event stream to begin sending events. Its sole parameter is the event stream to start.\nL99: \nL100: Once the event stream has been created and scheduled, if your run loop is not already running, you should start it by calling `【14†CFRunLoopRun】`.\nL101: ## Handling Events\nL102: \nL103: Your event handler callback must conform to the prototype for `【15†FSEventStreamCallback】`. The parameters are described in the reference documentation for the `FSEventStreamCallback` data type.\nL104: Your event handler receives three lists: a list of paths, a list of identifiers, and a list of flags. In effect, these represent a list of events. The first event consists of the first entry taken from each of the arrays, and so on. Your handler must iterate through these lists, processing the events as needed.\nL105: For each event, you should scan the directory at the specified path, processing its contents as desired. Normally, you need to scan only the exact directory specified by the path. However, there are three situations in which this is not the case:\nL106:   * If an event in a directory occurs at about the same time as one or more events in a subdirectory of that directory, the events may be coalesced into a single event. In this case, you will receive an event with the `【16†kFSEventStreamEventFlagMustScanSubDirs】` flag set. When you receive such an event, you must recursively rescan the path listed in the event. The additional changes are not necessarily in an immediate child of the listed path.\nL107:   * If a communication error occurs between the kernel and the user-space daemon, you may receive an event with either the `【17†kFSEventStreamEventFlagKernelDropped】` or `【18†kFSEventStreamEventFlagUserDropped】` flag set. In either case, you must do a full scan of any directories that you are monitoring because there is no way to determine what may have changed.\nL108: Note: When an event is dropped, the `【16†kFSEventStreamEventFlagMustScanSubDirs】` flag is also set. Thus, it is not necessary to explicitly check for the dropped event flags when determining whether to perform a full rescan of a path. The dropped event flags are provided purely for informational purposes.\nL109:   * If the root directory that you are watching is deleted, moved, or renamed (or if any of its parent directories are moved or renamed), the directory may cease to exist. If you care about this, you should pass the flag `【19†kFSEventStreamCreateFlagWatchRoot】` when creating the stream. In this case, you will receive an event with the flag `【20†kFSEventStreamEventFlagRootChanged】` and an event ID of zero (`0`). In this case, you must rescan the entire directory because it may not exist.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReadDirectoryChangesW function (winbase.h) - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-readdirectorychangesw)\nciteturn8view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn7view2\",\"lineno\":114}); Redirected to URL: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-readdirectorychangesw; Total lines: 208\nL102: A pointer to an 【7†OVERLAPPED】 structure that supplies data to be used during asynchronous operation. Otherwise, this value is NULL. The Offset and OffsetHigh members of this structure are not used.\nL103: \nL104: `[in, optional] lpCompletionRoutine`\nL105: \nL106: A pointer to a completion routine to be called when the operation has been completed or canceled and the calling thread is in an alertable wait state. For more information about this completion routine, see 【8†FileIOCompletionRoutine】.\nL107: ## Return value\nL108: \nL109: If the function succeeds, the return value is nonzero. For synchronous calls, this means that the operation succeeded. For asynchronous calls, this indicates that the operation was successfully queued.\nL110: \nL111: If the function fails, the return value is zero. To get extended error information, call 【9†GetLastError】.\nL112: \nL113: If the network redirector or the target file system does not support this operation, the function fails with ERROR_INVALID_FUNCTION.\nL114: ## Remarks\nL115: \nL116: To obtain a handle to a directory, use the 【10†CreateFile】 function with the FILE_FLAG_BACKUP_SEMANTICS flag.\nL117: \nL118: A call to ReadDirectoryChangesW can be completed synchronously or asynchronously. To specify asynchronous completion, open the directory with 【10†CreateFile】 as shown above, but additionally specify the FILE_FLAG_OVERLAPPED attribute in the dwFlagsAndAttributes parameter. Then specify an 【7†OVERLAPPED】 structure when you call ReadDirectoryChangesW.\nL119: When you first call ReadDirectoryChangesW, the system allocates a buffer to store change information. This buffer is associated with the directory handle until it is closed and its size does not change during its lifetime. Directory changes that occur between calls to this function are added to the buffer and then returned with the next call.\nL120: If the buffer overflows, ReadDirectoryChangesW will still return true, but the entire contents of the buffer are discarded and the lpBytesReturned parameter will be zero, which indicates that your buffer was too small to hold all of the changes that occurred.\nL121: Upon successful synchronous completion, the lpBuffer parameter is a formatted buffer and the number of bytes written to the buffer is available in lpBytesReturned. If the number of bytes transferred is zero, the buffer was either too large for the system to allocate or too small to provide detailed information on all the changes that occurred in the directory or subtree. In this case, you should compute the changes by enumerating the directory or subtree.\nL122: For asynchronous completion, you can receive notification in one of three ways:\nL123:   * Using the 【11†GetOverlappedResult】 function. To receive notification through GetOverlappedResult, do not specify a completion routine in the lpCompletionRoutine parameter. Be sure to set the hEvent member of the 【7†OVERLAPPED】 structure to a unique event.\nL124:   * Using the 【12†GetQueuedCompletionStatus】 function. To receive notification through GetQueuedCompletionStatus, do not specify a completion routine in lpCompletionRoutine. Associate the directory handle hDirectory with a completion port by calling the 【13†CreateIoCompletionPort】 function.\nL125:   * Using a completion routine. To receive notification through a completion routine, do not associate the directory with a completion port. Specify a completion routine in lpCompletionRoutine. This routine is called whenever the operation has been completed or canceled while the thread is in an alertable wait state. The hEvent member of the 【7†OVERLAPPED】 structure is not used by the system, so you can use it yourself.\nL126: For more information, see 【14†Synchronous and Asynchronous I/O】.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAlgorithm - Salsa (https://salsa-rs.github.io/salsa/reference/algorithm.html)\nciteturn8view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view0\",\"lineno\":55}); Total lines: 123\nL27:     5.       1. 【25†10.4.1. maybe changed after】\nL28:       2. 【26†10.4.2. Fetch】\nL29:       3. 【27†10.4.3. Derived queries flowchart】\nL30:       4. 【28†10.4.4. Cycle handling】\nL31:     6. 【29†10.5. Terminology】\nL32:     7.       1. 【30†10.5.1. Backdate】\nL33:       2. 【31†10.5.2. Changed at】\nL34:       3. 【32†10.5.3. Dependency】\nL35:       4. 【33†10.5.4. Derived query】\nL36:       5. 【34†10.5.5. Durability】\nL37:       6. 【35†10.5.6. Input query】\nL38:       7. 【36†10.5.7. Ingredient】\nL39:       8. 【37†10.5.8. LRU】\nL40:       9. 【38†10.5.9. Memo】\nL41:       10. 【39†10.5.10. Query】\nL42:       11. 【40†10.5.11. Query function】\nL43:       12. 【41†10.5.12. Revision】\nL44:       13. 【42†10.5.13. Salsa item】\nL45:       14. 【43†10.5.14. Salsa struct】\nL46:       15. 【44†10.5.15. Untracked dependency】\nL47:       16. 【45†10.5.16. Verified】\nL48:   19.   20. Appendices\nL49:   21. 【46†11. Meta: about the book itself】\nL50: # Salsa\nL51: \nL52: # 【47†The \"red-green\" algorithm】\nL53: \nL54: This page explains the basic Salsa incremental algorithm. The algorithm is called the \"red-green\" algorithm, which is where the name Salsa comes from.\nL55: ### 【48†Database revisions】\nL56: \nL57: The Salsa database always tracks a single revision. Each time you set an input, the revision is incremented. So we start in revision `R1`, but when a `set` method is called, we will go to `R2`, then `R3`, and so on. For each input, we also track the revision in which it was last changed.\nL58: ### 【49†Basic rule: when inputs change, re-execute!】\nL59: When you invoke a tracked function, in addition to storing the value that was returned, we also track what other tracked functions it depends on, and the revisions when their value last changed. When you invoke the function again, if the database is in a new revision, then we check whether any of the inputs to this function have changed in that new revision. If not, we can just return our cached value.\nL60: But if the inputs have changed (or may have changed), we will re-execute the function to find the most up-to-date answer.\nL61: Here is a simple example, where the `parse_module` function reads the module's text:\nL62: \nL63:     `#![allow(unused)]\nL64:     fn main() {\nL65:     #[salsa::input]\nL66:     struct Module {\nL67:         #[returns(deref)]\nL68:         text: String,\nL69:     }\nL70: \nL71:     #[salsa::tracked]\nL72:     fn parse_module(db: &dyn Db, module: Module) -> Ast {\nL73:         Ast::parse_text(module.text(db))\nL74:     }\nL75:     }`\nL76: If we invoke `parse_module` twice, but change the module text in between, then we will have to re-execute `parse_module`:\nL77: \nL78:     `#![allow(unused)]\nL79:     fn main() {\nL80:     use salsa::Setter as _;\nL81: \nL82:     let module = Module::new(&db, \"fn foo() { }\".to_string());\nL83:     parse_module(&db, module); // executes\nL84: \nL85:     // ...some time later...\nL86: \nL87:     module\nL88:         .set_text(&mut db)\nL89:         .to(\"fn foo() { /* add a comment */ }\".to_string());\nL90:     parse_module(&db, module); // executes again!\nL91:     }`\nL92: ### 【50†Backdating: sometimes we can be smarter】\nL93: \nL94: Often, though, tracked functions don't depend directly on the inputs. Instead, they'll depend on some other tracked function. For example, perhaps we have a `type_check` function that reads the AST:\nL95: \nL96:     `#![allow(unused)]\nL97:     fn main() {\nL98:     #[salsa::tracked]\nL99:     fn type_check(db: &dyn Db, module: Module) {\nL100:         let ast = parse_module(db, module);\nL101:         ...\nL102:     }\nL103:     }`\nL104: If the module text is changed, we saw that we have to re-execute `parse_module`, but there are many changes to the source text that still produce the same AST. For example, suppose we simply add a comment? In that case, if `type_check` is called again, we will:\nL105: \nL106:   * First re-execute `parse_module`, since its input changed.\nL107:   * We will then compare the resulting AST. If it's the same as last time, we can backdate the result, meaning that we say that, even though the inputs changed, the output didn't.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSkyframe - Bazel Documentation (https://bazel.build/reference/skyframe)\nciteturn8view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn5view1\",\"lineno\":85}); Total lines: 163\nL38: ### Build Encyclopedia\nL39: \nL40:   * 【6†Bazel BUILD Encyclopedia of Functions】\nL41:   * 【9†Common definitions】\nL42:   * 【10†Make Variables】\nL43:   * 【11†Functions】\nL44:   * 【12†Platforms and Toolchains Rules】\nL45:   * 【19†General Rules】\nL46:   * 【20†C / C++ Rules】\nL47:   * 【21†Java Rules】\nL48:   * 【22†Objective-C Rules】\nL49:   * 【23†Protocol Buffer Rules】\nL50:   * 【24†Python Rules】\nL51:   * 【25†Shell Rules】\nL52:   * 【26†Extra Actions Rules】\nL53:   * 【27†Be nav】\nL54: \nL55: ### Glossary\nL56: \nL57:   * 【28†Bazel Glossary】\nL58:   * 【29†Test encyclopedia】\nL59: ### Flag cheatsheet\nL60: \nL61:   * 【30†Bazel flag cheat sheet】\nL62: \nL63: ### Skyframe\nL64: \nL65:   * 【31†Skyframe】\nL66:   * 【32†Skyframe debugging】\nL67: \nL68: Skyframe\nL69: \nL70: # Skyframe\nL71: \nL72: The parallel evaluation and incrementality model of Bazel.\nL73: ##\nL74: \nL75: 【33†​】\nL76: \nL77: Data model\nL78: \nL79: The data model consists of the following items:\nL80:   * `SkyValue`. Also called nodes. `SkyValues` are immutable objects that contain all the data built over the course of the build and the inputs of the build. Examples are: input files, output files, targets and configured targets.\nL81:   * `SkyKey`. A short immutable name to reference a `SkyValue`, for example, `FILECONTENTS:/tmp/foo` or `PACKAGE://foo`.\nL82:   * `SkyFunction`. Builds nodes based on their keys and dependent nodes.\nL83:   * Node graph. A data structure containing the dependency relationship between nodes.\nL84:   * `Skyframe`. Code name for the incremental evaluation framework Bazel is based on.\nL85: ##\nL86: \nL87: 【34†​】\nL88: \nL89: Evaluation\nL90: A build is achieved by evaluating the node that represents the build request. First, Bazel finds the `SkyFunction` corresponding to the key of the top-level `SkyKey`. The function then requests the evaluation of the nodes it needs to evaluate the top-level node, which in turn result in other `SkyFunction` calls, until the leaf nodes are reached. Leaf nodes are usually ones that represent input files in the file system.\nL91: Finally, Bazel ends up with the value of the top-level `SkyValue`, some side effects (such as output files in the file system) and a directed acyclic graph of the dependencies between the nodes involved in the build. A `SkyFunction` can request `SkyKeys` in multiple passes if it cannot tell in advance all of the nodes it needs to do its job.\nL92: A simple example is evaluating an input file node that turns out to be a symlink: the function tries to read the file, realizes that it is a symlink, and thus fetches the file system node representing the target of the symlink. But that itself can be a symlink, in which case the original function will need to fetch its target, too. The functions are represented in the code by the interface `SkyFunction` and the services provided to it by an interface called `SkyFunction.Environment`.\nL93: These are the things functions can do:\nL94:   * Request the evaluation of another node by way of calling `env.getValue`. If the node is available, its value is returned, otherwise, `null` is returned and the function itself is expected to return `null`. In the latter case, the dependent node is evaluated, and then the original node builder is invoked again, but this time the same `env.getValue` call will return a non-`null` value.\nL95:   * Request the evaluation of multiple other nodes by calling `env.getValues()`. This does essentially the same, except that the dependent nodes are evaluated in parallel.\nL96:   * Do computation during their invocation\nL97:   * Have side effects, for example, writing files to the file system. Care needs to be taken that two different functions avoid stepping on each other’s toes. In general, write side effects (where data flows outwards from Bazel) are okay, read side effects (where data flows inwards into Bazel without a registered dependency) are not, because they are an unregistered dependency and as such, can cause incorrect incremental builds.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nclang: include/clang/DependencyScanning/DependencyScanningFilesystem.h Source File (https://clang.llvm.org/doxygen/DependencyScanningFilesystem_8h_source.html)\nciteturn8view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: op